हीं कर सकता। यही भगवान्\nकी पूजा का शुभारम्भ है। जैसाकि भगवद्गीता (७.१६) में पुष्टि की गई है—",
      "textEn": "336\n\nLeaves Amrut. Since I am very intelligent, why should I give this fool material prosperity? I\nSo I will inspire him to take the nectar of the refuge of my lotus feet and to forget the delusive material pleasures.\nFor that.\" If a devotee, along with any material desire, desires to\ndevoutly focus his mind on the lotus feet of Krishna, then Krishna can directly grant him pure devotion and\nwipe away all his material desires and possessions. This is a special\ngrace of God towards the devotees. Otherwise, if someone worships Krishna, but still wants to fulfill material desires, then he can be freed from all material desires just like Dhruva Maharaj, but\nit may take some time, but if a devout devotee only desires the lotus feet of Krishna,\nthen Krishna grants him pure devotion. Shri Aditiruvacha.\nKenaaham vidhinena brahmanu pasthasya jagatpatim.\nAs I have resolved, so shall my mind be. 22. Meaning of the word\n\nShri-Aditi said—Shrimati Aditi started praying; Kena—By whom; Aham—I; Vidhina—By the rules; Brahman—Hey\nBrahmin; Upasthasye—I can please; Jagat-patim—Lord of the universe, Jagannath; Yatha—From whom; Me—Mine; Satya-\nSankalpah—Desire fulfillment; Vidadhyat—Complete; Sahah—He; Manoratham—Desires, wishes. Mrs. Aditi said: Oh Brahmin! Tell me the rituals by which I can worship Jagannath,\nand may God be pleased with me and fulfill all my desires. Meaning: It is said, \"When one is conscious, one is not conscious of God immediately.\"\" In this way, a person may desire many\nthings, but until God fulfills these desires, they are not fulfilled. The fulfillment of desire is called Satyasankalpa. This word is very important.\nAditi left herself\nto her husband's mercy, so that she could give him such orders that he could fulfill all his desires by worshiping God. The disciple should first decide that I will\nworship God; only then will the guru give the right instructions to his disciple. No one can rule over their guru, just as a patient cannot ask a doctor for any special medicine. This is the beginning of the worship of God. As assured in Bhagavad Gita (7.16)—"
