      "textEn": "345\n\nNiveditam—offered prasad; Tat-bhaktay—to their devotee; Dadyat—to be given; Bhunjite—to eat; Va—or; Swayam—self;\nDattvam—giving; Achamanam—giving water for hand and face washing; Architva—worshiping; Tambulam—betel leaf; Cha—also;\nNivedet—to offer. He should give all the prasad or some part of it to a Vaishnav and then some\nPrasad should be accepted by himself. Thereafter, after performing Achaman to the Archa Vigraha, and then offering betel and betel nut,\nthen worship God again. I praise the Lord, who has been praised by the hymns. Having circumambulated the earth, I bow down to the staff. 42. Meaning of the word\n\nJapet—recite aloud; Ashtottara-shatam—one hundred and eight times; Stuvit—praise; Stuvitibhih—by praises of glory;\nPrabhum—to God; Kritva—having done; Pradakshinam—circumambulation; Bhumau—on the ground; Pranamet—bow down; Dandavat—bowing down prostrate; Muda—with joy. Thereafter, he should chant the mantra 10 times in his mouth and praise the glory of God.\nHe should offer prayers.\nThen he circumambulates the Lord and, in the end, with supreme satisfaction and joy,\nrolls on the ground (obeisances) and bows. Having made the head, he offered the rest to the deity. Dvyavaranbhojayedviprannpayasena yathothitam. 43. Meaning of the word\n\nKritva—by offering; Shirasi—on the head; Tat-Shesham—all the remaining (offerings to the deity); Devam—to the deity;\nUdvashayet—by taking to a sacred place and throwing away; Tat—thereafter; Dvi-Avaran—at least two;\nBhojayet—to feed; Vipran—to the Brahmins; Payasen—with kheer; Yatha-Uptam—as appropriate; Yatha-Uptam—as appropriate. After touching the water and all the flowers offered on the arch, throw them in some\nholy place. Then, at least two Brahmins should be fed kheer. Bhunjit, Tairanujitah, Seshah, Sesham, by the assembled. Brahmacharya, then, in that night, in the first day. 44). Having bathed, purified, and in the prescribed manner, he is well-acquainted.\nAfter bathing, one should perform the rituals. 45. Meaning of the word\n\nBhunjit—to accept the offering; Taah—from those Brahmins; Anumodah—with permission; Sa-ishtah—with friends and family;\nShesham—remaining; Sabhajitah—honored appropriately; Brahmachari—observing the vow of celibacy; Ath—indeed; Tat-"
